The attached image (Fig. 11 from the 2015 paper) illustrates the histological comparison of local inflammatory responses 2 weeks post-injection in mouse skeletal muscle for aluminum hydroxide (AH) adjuvant versus Nano-11 adjuvant. The left panel (AH) shows extensive granulomatous inflammation (asterisks) with numerous inflammatory cells infiltrating the muscle (M). In contrast, the right panel (Nano-11) reveals significantly less residual inflammation (asterisks) with fewer macrophages arranged in slender cords between muscle fibers. These results demonstrate that Nano-11 induces a more transient and less severe inflammatory response compared to AH, contributing to its improved safety profile as a vaccine adjuvant.

The image significantly contributes to vaccine development by providing direct visual evidence of the reduced local inflammatory response induced by Nano-11 compared to aluminum hydroxide. This finding supports Nano-11 as a safer adjuvant, as transient and less severe injection site reactions are highly desirable for improved patient compliance and the overall safety profile of new vaccines. This histological data is crucial for advancing Nano-11 as a viable candidate for next-generation vaccine formulations.